    "lawCode": "Amd §§2, 5 & 65, add §66-t, Pub Serv L; amd §§10 & 11, Transp Corps L; amd §224-d, Lab L; add §1020-ll, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9422\n\nSPONSOR: PARKER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, the transportation corporations\nlaw, the labor law and the public authorities law, in relation to ther-\nmal energy networks\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to remove the legal barriers to utility\ndevelopment of thermal energy networks and require the public service\ncommission to direct utilities to commence thermal energy network pilots\nin each major utility territory. This bill directs the public service\ncommission to develop a regulatory structure for utility thermal energy\nnetworks that scales affordable and accessible building electrification,\nprotects customers, and balances the role of incumbent monopoly utili-\nties with other market and public actors.  This bill ensures the devel-\nopment of and access to well trained, highly skilled craft persons need-\ned to support timely, reliable, high-quality thermal energy network\nprojects and promotes good jobs for local residents in the expanding\ndecarbonization sector.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Bill Title\n\nSection 2: Legislative findings\n\nSection 3: Definitions\n\nSection 4: Amends Subdivision 11 of section 2 of the public service law,\nas amended by chapter 159 of the laws of 1992. Changes the definition of\na gas utility such that they may own, operate, and manage thermal energy\nnetworks.\n\nSection 5: Amends Subdivision 13 of section 2 of the public service law,\nas amended by chapter 159 of the laws of 1992. Changes the definition of\nan electric utility such that they may own, operate, and manage thermal\nenergy networks.\n\nSection 6: Amends subdivision 1 of section 5 of public service law.\nExtends the jurisdiction, supervision, powers and duties of the public\nservice commission to thermal energy provided by gas corporations, elec-\ntric corporations, or combination gas and electric corporations.\n\nSection 7: Amends paragraphs (c) and (d) of subdivision 6 of section 65\nof the public service law, paragraph (c) as amended by chapter 204 of\nthe laws of 2010 and paragraph (d) as amended by chapter 388 of the laws\nof 2011. Allows utilities to charge for the provision of thermal energy\nservice.\n\nSection 8: Amends Article 2 Section 10 of the Transportation Corpo-\nrations Law. Changes the definition of gas and electric utilities such\nthat they may acquire and supply thermal energy.\n\nSection 9: Amends Article 2 Section 11 of the Transportation Corpo-\nrations Law. Authorizes gas and electric utilities to sell and furnish\nthermal energy for heating or cooling and to lay conductors, pipes,\nconduits, ducts and other fixture for thermal energy networks in public\nrights of way and authorizes gas utilities to provide thermal energy in\nareas where natural gas has become inadequate or insufficient to give\nreasonable service to consumers.\n\nSection 10: Amends 224-D of Labor Law to apply prevailing wage and\ndirect entry pre-apprenticeship requirements to thermal energy projects.\n\nSection 11: Amends public service law by adding a new section 66-t.\nDirects the public service commission to initiate a proceeding to\nsupport the development of thermal energy networks for the purpose of\nmeeting the greenhouse gas emissions and equity goals of the Climate\nLeadership and Community Protection Act. Requires the commission to\nconsider the various ownership, market, and rate structures for thermal\nenergy networks and whether the provision of thermal energy services by\ngas and/or electric utilities is in the public interest.  Sets forth a\nprocess by which the seven largest gas, electric, or combination gas and\nelectric corporations shall propose thermal energy network pilot\nprojects and the public service commission to consider for approval the\nthermal energy network pilot projects, including in disadvantaged commu-\nnities. Prioritizes, for the operations and maintenance of thermal ener-\ngy networks, transitioning utility workers who have lost, or are at risk\nof losing, their employment with a utility downsizing its gas trans-\nmission and distribution system.\n\nSection 12. Amends the public authorities by adding a new section 1020-\nLL. Requires the Long Island Power Authority to develop thermal energy\nnetwork pilots and to promulgate regulations for thermal energy networks\nconsistent with the standards set forth for the public service commis-\nsion in this act.\n\nSection 13. Establishes that the act takes effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's Climate Leadership and Community Protection Act requires the\ndecarbonization of the building sector, which in turn requires decarbon-\nization of the heating and hot water services currently supplied by\nutilities. Public service law is outdated in that utilities may deliver\nelectricity, gas or steam service, but \"thermal energy\" and infrastruc-\nture that can deliver that energy is not contemplated, creating a lack\nof clarity in the law as to whether utilities may construct, own, and\nmaintain thermal energy networks.  Thermal energy networks are pipe\nloops that connect multiple buildings together with each other and with\nsources of thermal energy like geothermal boreholes, surface water, and\nwastewater. The pipes in thermal energy networks carry water or other\nnon-combustible liquids to conduct thermal energy. These networks repre-\nsent an important strategy to help New York scale building electrifica-\ntion and decarbonization, reduce costs for customers, minimize impacts\non the electric grid, and retain and create good union jobs.\n\nThe bill updates the law to clarify that utilities may deliver thermal\nenergy services through thermal energy networks constructed by a skilled\nworkforce that is paid a prevailing wage and that supports training and\nentry by local residents. The bill also sets forth a regulatory process\nfor piloting thermal energy networks and developing appropriate regu-\nlations for thermal energy networks that will support achievement of the\nclimate justice and greenhouse gas emissions reductions mandated by the\nClimate Leadership and Community Protection Act.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
